+## Disposition: ACCEPT as a rigorous METHOD-SCOPE NO-GO (negative horn)
+
+Alternation trading cannot yield an unconditional fixed-polynomial lower bound
+`SORT-BOUND-U ∉ DTIME(N^c)` for any fixed c>1: the method's structural template
+requires a completeness handle and this sparse tally language has none usable.
+This closes the method family as a route to this single language. It is a
+legitimate barrier result (AGENTS.md: "barriers that rule out whole families of
+methods"), NOT a theorem completion. S(n)/SORT-BOUND-U remain OPEN.
+
+### Ground 0 — encoding fidelity (verified by re-derivation)
+
+`SORT-BOUND-U ∈ DTIME(N^c)` for fixed c ⟺ `S(n)` computable in poly(n) time.
+(⟹) `S(n) ≤ n(n−1)/2`, so binary search k over [0, n(n−1)/2]: each probe has
+`N ≤ n + n(n−1)/2 + 1 = O(n²)`, cost `O(n^{2c})`, `O(log n)` probes ⇒ `O(n^{2c} log n)`
+= poly(n). (⟸) compute S(n), compare to k. Correct. So the front is exactly
+"S(n) is not polynomial-time computable" in decision clothing; no new class
+separation follows either way. Consistent with the 20:14Z/20:23Z Fourier
+NO-GO delimitation.
+
+### Ground 1 — method structure (verified)
+
+Alternation trading (W. Williams, CCC 2007; on Chandra–Kozen–Stockmeyer
+`ATIME = PSPACE` and time hierarchies) is a class-inclusion technique: assume
+`L ∈ DTIME(n^c)`, use `L`'s `≤_m^p`-completeness for a class C (reduction
+exponent r) to get `C ⊆ DTIME(n^{cr})`, trade alternation blocks for time, then
+contradict an unconditional hierarchy. Step (ii) is the sole conversion of a
+single-language assumption into a class-wide statement; hierarchies assert a
+language in `C \ C'`, never a property of one language. Hence a completeness
+handle is structurally necessary. Correct characterization of the method.
+
+### Ground 2 — no usable handle (verified against canonical sources)
+
+- **NP:** a sparse set many-one NP-hard ⇒ P=NP (Mahaney 1982; canonical
+  `research-decision-encoding.md` Prop 5.1, `research-certificates-barriers.md`
+  Claim B). SORT-BOUND-U is sparse (`problem.md:81`), so an NP handle is
+  unavailable unconditionally; `NP ⊄ DTIME(n^c)` is itself equivalent to P≠NP
+  (if `NP ⊆ DTIME(n^c)` for a fixed c then NP=P via the reduction closure).
+  The reporter's careful distinction (not the false "NP-hard ⇒ in P") is right.
+- **PSPACE:** the live ledger front `sort-bound-u-pspace-complete` records an
+  unconditional sparse-hard obstruction (sparse many-one PSPACE-hard ⇒
+  `PSPACE ⊆ P`, contra `P ⊊ PSPACE`), so no many-one PSPACE handle; Turing
+  hardness yields only `PSPACE ⊆ P/poly` (Karp–Lipton), no contradiction.
+  Canonical docs establish only `∈ PSPACE`, not completeness — the reporter
+  correctly attributes the collapse to the ledger front, not to a canonical
+  theorem, and flags it as not re-derived. Sound as a barrier reference.
+- **Time classes:** alternation trading needs near-linear reduction exponent r;
+  no `≤_m^p`-completeness of SORT-BOUND-U for any DTIME(n^a)/NTIME(n^a) is known
+  (tally + sparse-hard obstructions). Correct.
+
+### Ground 3 — distinctions and falsification attempts (verified)
+
+Sparsity argument correct: a tally language carries one bit per length; a
+completeness handle would have to compress a whole class into poly(N) bits,
+which sparse hard-set collapses rule out absent a class collapse. General-poset
+hardness (Hyafil–Rivest, Laber–Nogueira, Parberry) uses different models and
+would itself need a reduction back to SORT-BOUND-U (a handle) to transfer —
+consistent with `research-hardness-boundaries.md`. Soundness vs completeness
